{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And let them encamp.</b> I do not want the Egyptians to hold them to the terms they set for themselves — a journey of three days into the wilderness." "<b>Before Pi-hahiroth.</b> This is Pithom, and now it is called Pi-hahiroth because they became free there, and the Egyptians could not contest Israel's freedom, as stated in the midrash: such was the custom of the Egyptians — any slave who fled from his master, once he reached Pi-hahiroth, which is Pithom, immediately became a free man. Another interpretation: <b>before Pi-hahiroth</b> — to mislead Pharaoh, so that he would think they are bewildered in the land and regret having left, when they turn back before Pi-hahiroth, which is Pithom, the place of their enslavement." "<b>Opposite it.</b> Opposite Pi-hahiroth. <b>And you shall encamp by the sea</b> — and when Pharaoh comes, he will see your banner."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:57:42.120195475Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}